1. What is WebRTC?
WebRTC = Web Real-Time Communication.
WebRTC is a set of technologies/APIs and protocols that enables real-time audio, video, and data communication between clients.

It is commonly used for:
Video calls
Audio calls
Screen sharing
Peer-to-peer data

WebRTC handles important things such as media capture, encoding/decoding, encryption, network traversal, and real-time media transport.
2. Simple 1-to-1 Video Call
Suppose:
User A 📱  ←────────→  💻 User B

Ideally, we want media to travel directly:
User A
   │
   │ Audio + Video
   │
   ▼
User B

User A
   ▲
   │ Audio + Video
   │
User B

This is Peer-to-Peer (P2P) communication.
After the connection is established, the media path can be:
        WebRTC
User A ═════════ User B
        P2P

So your video doesn't necessarily need to travel through an application server.
But establishing that connection is where things get interesting.
3. How do the two users connect?
Suppose A wants to call B.
They first need to exchange connection information.
For this, we use a signaling server.
User A
   │
   │
   ▼
Signaling Server
   │
   │
   ▼
User B

The signaling server helps exchange information required to establish the WebRTC connection.
For example:
A → "I want to call B"

A → connection/media information
        ↓
Signaling Server
        ↓
B

B → connection/media information
        ↓
Signaling Server
        ↓
A

WebRTC itself does not define a mandatory signaling protocol. You can implement signaling using WebSocket, HTTP, etc.
4. SDP
You'll hear SDP when studying WebRTC.
SDP = Session Description Protocol.
It describes things such as:
I support these codecs
I want to send audio
I want to send video
Here is media/network information

Very simplified flow:
User A
   │
   │ SDP Offer
   ▼
Signaling Server
   │
   ▼
User B
   │
   │ SDP Answer
   ▼
Signaling Server
   │
   ▼
User A

This is often called:
Offer / Answer

You don't need SDP internals for HLD.
5. But how do they find each other over the internet?
Here's the next problem.
Your laptop might have a private IP:
192.168.x.x

because you're behind a router/NAT.
Laptop
   │
   ▼
Router / NAT
   │
   ▼
Internet

User B cannot simply connect to your private IP.
This is where ICE, STUN and TURN come in.
6. STUN Server
STUN helps a client discover its public-facing IP/port mapping and assists WebRTC in finding a viable direct connection path.
Conceptually:
User A
   │
   │ "What address do others see me as?"
   ▼
STUN Server
   │
   ▼
Public IP/port information

Then WebRTC attempts to establish a direct connection.
User A ═════════ User B

STUN is generally lightweight because it doesn't normally carry the actual ongoing video stream.
7. What if P2P doesn't work?
Some networks/firewalls/NAT configurations prevent a direct connection.
Then WebRTC can use a TURN server.
TURN acts as a relay.
Instead of:
A ═════════ B

we get:
A
 ╲
  ╲ Video/Audio
   ▼
 TURN Server
   │
   │ Video/Audio
   ▼
   B

So:
STUN
→ Helps establish/discover a direct path

TURN
→ Relays media when direct connectivity fails

TURN is more expensive because actual audio/video traffic passes through it.
8. Complete 1-to-1 architecture
Now put everything together:
             Signaling Server
             /              \
            /                \
           ▼                  ▼
       User A                User B
          │                    │
          │      STUN          │
          └────── Server ──────┘
          
             Try direct P2P

       User A ═════════════ User B
                WebRTC

If direct connection fails:
User A
   │
   ▼
TURN Server
   │
   ▼
User B

So for 1-to-1 calls, P2P can be a good architecture.
9. Now the problem: Group video calls
Suppose there are 4 users:
A
B
C
D

One possibility is for everyone to connect directly to everyone.
This is called a mesh architecture.
      A
    / | \
   /  |  \
  B---+---C
   \  |  /
    \ | /
      D

A sends video to:
A → B
A → C
A → D

B:
B → A
B → C
B → D

etc.
For N users, each participant may need to upload their stream to approximately:
N - 1 users

That's the big problem.
Imagine:
10 participants

Each user potentially uploads
9 copies of their video 😵

That's a lot of bandwidth and CPU/network overhead.
So pure P2P mesh doesn't scale well for larger calls.


!10. Enter SFU
SFU = Selective Forwarding Unit.
An SFU is a media server that receives audio/video streams from participants and selectively forwards those streams to other participants.

Instead of:
A → B
A → C
A → D

A sends roughly one upstream stream/set of encoded layers toward the SFU:
A
 │
 ▼
SFU

Same for everyone:
          ┌─────┐
     A ──►│     │──► A
     B ──►│ SFU │──► B
     C ──►│     │──► C
     D ──►│     │──► D
          └─────┘

More accurately:
        A ─────┐
               │
        B ─────┤
               ▼
             SFU
               │
        C ─────┤
               │
        D ─────┘

SFU receives the streams and forwards appropriate streams to participants.
11. Example
Suppose:
A = Nishchay
B = Rahul
C = Amit
D = Priya

You send your video:
Nishchay
    │
    │ video stream
    ▼
   SFU

SFU can forward it to:
             ┌──→ Rahul
Nishchay → SFU──→ Amit
             └──→ Priya

You don't need to upload three separate copies yourself.
That saves a lot of client upload bandwidth.
12. Why "Selective" Forwarding?
Because the SFU doesn't necessarily forward every stream at maximum quality to every user.
Suppose 100 people are in a meeting.
You don't need 100 HD videos simultaneously.
Maybe your screen shows:
Speaker → HD

6 visible participants → Medium quality

Others → Low quality / no video

The SFU can select which streams/layers to forward.
A common technique is simulcast, where the sender provides multiple quality layers:
User A
  │
  ├── High quality
  ├── Medium quality
  └── Low quality
        ↓
       SFU

Then:
Fast internet user
← High quality

Average connection
← Medium quality

Slow connection
← Low quality

This is one reason SFUs are powerful for conferencing.
13. Does SFU process/mix everyone's video?
Usually an SFU primarily forwards media rather than decoding and combining everything into one video.
There's another architecture called MCU.
MCU = Multipoint Control Unit.
Conceptually:
A ──┐
B ──┤
C ──┼──→ MCU → Mix/combine → Single stream
D ──┘

MCU may decode/process/mix streams and generate combined output.
This requires significantly more server-side computation.
SFU:
Receive
   ↓
Select
   ↓
Forward

MCU:
Receive
   ↓
Decode
   ↓
Mix/process
   ↓
Encode
   ↓
Send

Modern real-time conferencing architectures commonly make heavy use of SFU-style approaches, though actual products can use more complex/hybrid designs.
P2P vs SFU vs MCU
	P2P / Mesh	SFU	MCU
Media path	Client ↔ Client	Client → SFU → Clients	Client → MCU → Clients
Server media processing	Minimal	Mostly forwarding/selecting	Heavy processing
Client upload	Increases with participants	Much lower	Low
Server bandwidth	Low	High	High
Server CPU	Low	Moderate	High
Good for	Small/1:1 calls	Group conferencing	Server-mixed media


14. Zoom/Meet-style HLD
Don't memorize that a specific commercial product uses exactly this simplified architecture everywhere. But an SFU-based video-conferencing design could look like:
                     Users
                       │
                       ▼
                 API Gateway
                       │
              ┌────────┴────────┐
              ▼                 ▼
        Meeting Service    User Service
              │
              ▼
       Signaling Service
              │
              ▼
         SFU Cluster
       /    /   \    \
      ▼    ▼     ▼    ▼
     U1   U2     U3   U4

Media flow:
                  SFU
              ↗    ↑    ↖
             /     |     \
            /      |      \
           A       B       C

       Audio/Video streams

Control/signaling and media are conceptually different:
Signaling
─────────
Join meeting
Leave meeting
SDP/ICE information
Mute/unmute state
Connection setup


Media
─────
Audio
Video
Screen sharing

That's a very important HLD distinction.
15. Full mental model
Remember this chain:
              Video Calling
                    │
                   WebRTC
                    │
        ┌───────────┴───────────┐
        │                       │
    Connection              Media
     Setup
        │
   ┌────┼────┐
   │    │    │
Signaling STUN TURN
              │
              ▼
         Media path
              │
       ┌──────┴──────┐
       │             │
     1-to-1        Group
       │             │
      P2P           SFU

For an HLD interview, remember these six terms:
WebRTC → real-time audio/video/data communication stack
Signaling → exchanges information needed to establish the connection
STUN → helps discover connectivity/public mapping
TURN → relays media when direct connection isn't possible
P2P → clients communicate directly; useful for small/1:1 scenarios
SFU → receives and selectively forwards media; useful for scalable group calls
If you understand this flow, you already have enough foundation to explain why a 1-to-1 calling system 
and a 50-person meeting system need different architectures.